;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE TRIALS BACKGROUND — [TrialsBgDef]
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The mode's own backdrop, drawn over the stage and behind everything this module puts
; on screen. It is a full background definition — the same format a stage or a title
; screen uses — so anything the engine's background elements can do, it can do here:
; scrolling, sin movement, velocity, tiling, multiple layered elements.
;
; NOT SHIPPED, AND NOT REQUIRED. No section, no backdrop, and the mode draws its Steps
; straight over the stage, which is what a stock install looks like. This exists for a
; screenpack that wants the mode to have an identity of its own.
;
; DEFINE IT IN YOUR OWN system.def, not here. The module reads it out of whichever
; layer declares it, and reads it with the ENGINE's own parser rather than through the
; merged config: it is handed the file and the section name, and parses the whole block
; itself. That is why it survives at all — the engine's motif struct has no field for
; this section and drops it, but nothing about that reaches the parser underneath.
;
; WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE, in your system.def:
;
;   [TrialsBgDef]
;   spr = trials.sff        ; optional; your screenpack's own sff is used without it
;
;   [TrialsBg Panel]
;   type  = normal
;   spriteno = 100, 0
;   start = 0, 0
;   layerno = 0             ; 0 draws under the Steps, 1 over them
;
;   [TrialsBg Frame]
;   type  = normal
;   spriteno = 100, 1
;   start = 0, 0
;   layerno = 1
;
; Element sections are [TrialsBg <name>] — the engine matches on the prefix, so the
; name after it is yours. Refer to the Elecbyte background documentation for the full
; element vocabulary; every key of it is available.
;
; EXPECT CONSOLE WARNINGS. The engine parses your system.def into its own motif struct
; before this module ever sees the file, and that struct has no field for these
; sections — so it logs "Failed to assign key [trialsbgdef.spr]", and one more per key
; of every element section. They are harmless and unavoidable: the same lines appear for
; [Trials Mode] itself, and the background works regardless, because this module reads
; the file with the engine's background parser rather than through the motif.
;
; bgclearcolor is parsed and deliberately ignored. Clearing the screen is what it does,
; and this background is drawn over a live match — honouring it would erase the stage.
;
; NOT the pause menu's background. That is [TrialsPauseBgDef], which the engine resolves
; natively from the game mode name; you do not need this module for it.
;
; IF YOU ALREADY HAD ONE, CHECK IT. Before the rebuild this section backed the module's
; own trials MENU screen, which was a full screen of its own and is gone — the pause
; menu is the engine's now. So a [TrialsBgDef] carried over from then is full-screen
; artwork that will be drawn over a live match, and will cover the stage. Either give
; its elements positions and a size that suit a backdrop drawn over a fight, or move
; them to [TrialsPauseBgDef], where the old full-screen reading still holds.

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE MODULE'S OWN ACTIONS — [Begin Action N]
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; One action per sprite trials.sff ships, numbered to match it: action 20 draws sprite
; 0,20. Any element in [Trials Mode] above can be moved from spr = 0, N to anim = N
; without a line of artwork being drawn first, which is what these are for.
;
; NOT AN ANIMATION ANYBODY WOULD SHIP. Each is the one sprite it names held at full
; alpha and then at half, so what it demonstrates is that frame timing and per-frame
; blending reach the screen. The shipped defaults use the sprites, not these — a
; screenpack that never mentions an action number is drawn exactly as it was before
; these existed.
;
; DEFINE YOUR OWN IN YOUR OWN system.def, not here. An action number in your file is
; read out of your file and drawn out of your sff; one in this file is read out of this
; file and drawn out of trials.sff. That is the same rule sprite numbers follow, and
; there is no way to cross it in either direction.
;
; THE ENGINE NEVER READS THIS FILE. It reads every screenpack's actions itself, and it
; reads the module's `+system.def` beside this one, but this file is the module's alone
; — read with the engine's own action parser, handed trials.sff. So nothing here can
; collide with a screenpack's numbering, however plain the numbers look.
;
; FRAME FORMAT is the engine's own: group, index, xoffset, yoffset, time, flip, blend.
; AS128D128 is the ordinary half-transparency spelling. Keep every frame of one action
; the same size — a background is measured once, from its first frame.
; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

; The panel behind the whole Step list, vertical Layout.
[Begin Action 0]
0,0, 0,0, 20
0,0, 0,0, 20, , AS128D128

; The same, horizontal Layout.
[Begin Action 1]
0,1, 0,0, 20
0,1, 0,0, 20, , AS128D128

; The plate under one Step, vertical Layout.
[Begin Action 10]
0,10, 0,0, 20
0,10, 0,0, 20, , AS128D128

; The arrow's shaft, horizontal Layout. Stretched along x to the Step's own width, so
; this and the two below are the three pieces of one graphic and animate together.
[Begin Action 20]
0,20, 0,0, 20
0,20, 0,0, 20, , AS128D128

; Its nock.
[Begin Action 21]
0,21, 0,0, 20
0,21, 0,0, 20, , AS128D128

; Its point.
[Begin Action 22]
0,22, 0,0, 20
0,22, 0,0, 20, , AS128D128
